13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1a",null,{"guide":{"date":"April 22–November 5, 1861","place":"Richmond and Harpers Ferry, Virginia; the northern Shenandoah Valley from Williamsport and Falling Waters through Martinsburg and Winchester; Ashby’s Gap and Piedmont; and Manassas Junction and Henry Hill, Virginia","result":"Jackson converted Virginia volunteers at Harpers Ferry into the First Virginia Brigade, served under Joseph E. Johnston, conducted a fighting withdrawal at Falling Waters, and led his brigade in Johnston’s rapid movement to Manassas. The brigade’s stand and counterattack on Henry Hill helped produce the Confederate victory at First Manassas and gave Jackson and the brigade the name “Stonewall.” Promoted to major general in October, Jackson left the brigade on November 4 and assumed independent command in the Shenandoah Valley at Winchester on November 5","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"Jackson’s first six months of Civil War command","note":"The narrative begins with his April 1861 Virginia commission and ends with his assumption of command at Winchester. First Manassas is summarized rather than retold in detail."},{"label":"Harpers Ferry command","value":"Colonel of Virginia forces; arrived April 28, 1861","note":"Jackson found an unorganized concentration of volunteers and militia and imposed drill, discipline, and a brigade structure. Sources differ by one day on whether his command technically began April 27 or April 28."},{"label":"Why Harpers Ferry mattered","value":"Armory, railroad, canal, river crossings, and the northern entrance to the Valley","note":"Its industrial and transportation value was considerable, but surrounding heights made the town itself difficult to defend against artillery."},{"label":"Maryland Heights","value":"Jackson occupied the commanding height in May 1861","note":"The National Park Service identifies this as one of Jackson’s earliest aggressive command decisions. It also created political risk because Maryland had not seceded."},{"label":"First Virginia Brigade","value":"2nd, 4th, 5th, 27th, and 33rd Virginia Infantry with the Rockbridge Artillery","note":"The formation became the Stonewall Brigade after First Manassas. The episode concentrates on Jackson’s training and discipline rather than enumerating every regiment."},{"label":"B&O railroad seizure","value":"The scale and method are disputed","note":"The traditional Imboden story says Jackson manipulated train schedules and trapped 56 locomotives and more than 300 cars. The U.S. Army Center of Military History repeats the large seizure, but biographer James I. Robertson Jr., as the episode notes, rejected Imboden’s elaborate account for lack of contemporary documentation. Confederate interference with the B&O and seizures of railroad property are not in dispute."},{"label":"Little Sorrel","value":"Jackson’s small sorrel gelding and best-known wartime mount","note":"The episode links the horse to railroad property seized near Harpers Ferry and says Jackson paid his quartermaster for it. The exact acquisition story depends on later recollections and should not be treated as fully documented."},{"label":"Johnston takes command","value":"May 23–24, 1861","note":"The Army history dates Johnston’s assumption of command to May 23; Encyclopedia Virginia gives May 24. Jackson initially required written evidence of Johnston’s authority, then accepted command of the First Brigade under him."},{"label":"Withdrawal from Harpers Ferry","value":"June 14, 1861","note":"Johnston considered Winchester more defensible and more important to the Valley. Confederate troops destroyed government and railroad property as they left."},{"label":"Falling Waters / Hoke’s Run","value":"July 2, 1861; United States tactical success","note":"Jackson’s mission was to determine Patterson’s strength and retire if the United States force was substantial. His official report says he accomplished that mission and withdrew in order; the Army history describes Patterson as brushing the Confederates aside. Both descriptions fit a limited reconnaissance followed by a Confederate retreat."},{"label":"Jackson’s force engaged","value":"380 men of the 5th Virginia and one artillery piece","note":"Jackson’s official report says three of Pendleton’s four guns were left behind. Two additional regiments moved in support but did not enter the action."},{"label":"Falling Waters losses","value":"Contemporary and later totals conflict","note":"Jackson reported 11 wounded and 9 missing in the 5th Virginia, several of the missing believed dead; Stuart reported 1 wounded and 2 missing. He also claimed 49 United States prisoners. The episode’s rounded statement of about two dozen Confederate casualties and a similar United States total should not be combined with later, higher estimates as if they were one settled count."},{"label":"Promotion to brigadier general","value":"Appointment dated June 17; news reached Jackson after Falling Waters","note":"The episode places receipt of the commission a few days after the July 2 skirmish. The formal appointment date was earlier."},{"label":"March to Piedmont","value":"About 20 miles in 14 hours, July 18–19","note":"The episode traces the brigade from Winchester through Millwood, a Shenandoah River ford, Ashby’s Gap, and Paris, followed by another six-mile march to the railroad at Piedmont."},{"label":"Rail movement to Manassas","value":"About 34 miles from Piedmont; Jackson arrived July 19","note":"Johnston’s infantry used the Manassas Gap Railroad while cavalry and artillery moved overland. This concentration helped the two Confederate armies meet McDowell on July 21."},{"label":"First Manassas","value":"Confederate victory, July 21, 1861","note":"Jackson’s roughly 2,500 Virginians formed on Henry Hill. The episode emphasizes his initiative, the brigade’s stand, and its counterattack; the full battle was covered in earlier podcast episodes."},{"label":"“Stonewall” quotation","value":"Attributed to Brig. Gen. Barnard E. Bee Jr.","note":"The wording varies among accounts, and some contemporary evidence allows the remark to have been critical rather than purely complimentary. Bee was mortally wounded before he could explain it."},{"label":"Tactical ideas emphasized","value":"Bayonet action, flank attacks, concentration, secrecy, and surprise","note":"The episode presents Jackson’s preference for the bayonet as increasingly outdated against rifled muskets, while identifying flank attack and concentration against a weak point as more consequential parts of his method."},{"label":"Independent Valley command","value":"Promoted October 7; assigned district command October 28; reached Winchester November 5","note":"Jackson bid farewell to the First Brigade on November 4. The episode’s “6,000-square-mile district” is a descriptive estimate, not a surveyed administrative area."},{"label":"Book recommendation","value":"Rebel Yell by S. C. Gwynne","note":"The transcript renders the author’s surname incorrectly. The full title is Rebel Yell: The Violence, Passion, and Redemption of Stonewall Jackson."}],"maps":[{"title":"Harpers Ferry, Falling Waters, and the Valley line","caption":"Jackson organized and drilled the First Virginia Brigade at Harpers Ferry before Johnston withdrew the army to Winchester. On July 2 Patterson crossed at Williamsport. Jackson advanced from near Martinsburg with the 5th Virginia and one gun, skirmished at Falling Waters, and retired through his forward camp toward Big Spring. Geography and movements are schematic; the map does not claim exact unit frontages.","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-2.pdf","image":"/images/ep139-harpers-ferry-falling-waters.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Harpers Ferry, Winchester, Martinsburg, Falling Waters, and Patterson and Jackson movements on July 2, 1861"},{"title":"The First Brigade moves from Winchester to Manassas","caption":"On July 18 Jackson’s brigade led Johnston’s movement from Winchester to Millwood, forded the Shenandoah, crossed the Blue Ridge at Ashby’s Gap, and halted near Paris about 2 a.m. After reaching Piedmont on foot, the infantry traveled roughly 34 miles by the Manassas Gap Railroad and arrived at Manassas Junction on July 19. The route and distances are schematic.","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-2.pdf","image":"/images/ep139-valley-to-manassas.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Jackson’s July 18 and 19 forced march and rail movement from Winchester through Ashby’s Gap and Piedmont to Manassas Junction"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:26","audioTime":86,"title":"The story resumes in April 1861","detail":"Jackson and 176 VMI cadets reached Richmond, where the cadets trained Virginia recruits and Jackson received a colonel’s commission."},{"time":"2:10","audioTime":130,"title":"Jackson is ordered to Harpers Ferry","detail":"The episode introduces the armory town at the confluence of the Potomac and Shenandoah, on the B&O Railroad and beside the Chesapeake and Ohio Canal."},{"time":"3:29","audioTime":209,"title":"The position’s defensive weakness","detail":"Surrounding heights dominated the low town. Jackson nevertheless worked to fortify the area and occupied Maryland Heights during his command."},{"time":"3:55","audioTime":235,"title":"An unorganized garrison awaits him","detail":"The episode estimates about 2,500 volunteers and militiamen at Harpers Ferry, with little organization above company level."},{"time":"5:10","audioTime":310,"title":"Jackson imposes order and training","detail":"Militia reorganization removed supernumerary senior officers. Jackson drilled the recruits, enforced regulations, and helped form the First Virginia Brigade."},{"time":"6:57","audioTime":417,"title":"Secrecy becomes a command habit","detail":"The hosts connect Jackson’s reserve and secrecy to his desire to surprise the enemy, while noting that subordinates were often left without enough information."},{"time":"7:53","audioTime":473,"title":"Discipline is enforced by formal charges","detail":"Jackson expected literal compliance and regularly used arrest and court-martial proceedings against soldiers who left their posts or failed to perform duty."},{"time":"9:01","audioTime":541,"title":"The B&O becomes a military target","detail":"The railroad connected the Midwest and the East Coast and carried traffic across the Potomac at Harpers Ferry, making it strategically valuable to both sides."},{"time":"9:55","audioTime":595,"title":"Lee urges a defensive course","detail":"The episode quotes Lee’s May 12 warning about Maryland opinion. Jackson treated the qualifying phrase “if possible” as room for aggressive action."},{"time":"11:01","audioTime":661,"title":"The locomotive story is introduced and challenged","detail":"Imboden’s later story credits Jackson with trapping 56 locomotives and more than 300 cars. The episode immediately notes Robertson’s conclusion that this elaborate version was fictional."},{"time":"11:38","audioTime":698,"title":"Jackson acquires Little Sorrel","detail":"The episode links the gelding to a documented seizure of a train, says Jackson paid for the horse, and explains why its endurance suited his later campaigning."},{"time":"13:47","audioTime":827,"title":"Joseph E. Johnston supersedes Jackson","detail":"Jackson initially refused to yield without written authority. Once Johnston produced documentation, Jackson accepted brigade command in the Army of the Shenandoah."},{"time":"14:57","audioTime":897,"title":"Johnston withdraws toward Winchester","detail":"Facing Patterson’s larger force, Johnston abandoned Harpers Ferry in mid-June and sent Jackson forward toward Martinsburg with the First Brigade."},{"time":"16:46","audioTime":1006,"title":"Patterson crosses the Potomac","detail":"On July 2 United States troops crossed near Williamsport. Stuart’s message reached Jackson outside Martinsburg at about 7:30 a.m."},{"time":"17:22","audioTime":1042,"title":"Jackson begins a reconnaissance in force","detail":"Kenton Harper’s 5th Virginia moved toward Falling Waters with William Pendleton’s battery; Jackson took only one of its guns into the skirmish."},{"time":"17:58","audioTime":1078,"title":"Skirmishers open the action","detail":"The Confederates fired from partly covered positions near the road. United States skirmishers recoiled, re-formed with supports, and pressed forward."},{"time":"18:46","audioTime":1126,"title":"Pendleton’s gun checks the pursuit","detail":"A solid shot cleared the road and helped cover the Confederate retirement. The episode’s remembered benediction before firing is an anecdote, not part of Jackson’s official report."},{"time":"19:08","audioTime":1148,"title":"Stuart captures United States soldiers","detail":"Separated temporarily from his cavalry, Stuart approached soldiers while still wearing a blue prewar uniform. Jackson’s official report lists 49 prisoners taken by Stuart’s command."},{"time":"19:52","audioTime":1192,"title":"Jackson reaches Big Spring","detail":"The reconnaissance ended with Jackson about two and a half miles south of Martinsburg. Patterson occupied Martinsburg the next day."},{"time":"21:04","audioTime":1264,"title":"Jackson receives brigadier general’s rank","detail":"The news reached him after Falling Waters, although the formal Confederate appointment was dated June 17."},{"time":"21:22","audioTime":1282,"title":"Johnston orders the movement to Manassas","detail":"On July 18 the Army of the Shenandoah began evading Patterson to reinforce Beauregard against McDowell."},{"time":"22:53","audioTime":1373,"title":"The First Brigade crosses the Blue Ridge","detail":"After an eleven-mile march to Millwood, the troops crossed the Shenandoah and Ashby’s Gap, reaching Paris at about 2 a.m. after nearly twenty miles."},{"time":"23:35","audioTime":1415,"title":"A sentry story is labeled as legend","detail":"The episode says legend has Jackson allowing his exhausted men to sleep while he stood guard. No firm contemporary evidence is supplied for the story."},{"time":"23:47","audioTime":1427,"title":"The brigade boards trains at Piedmont","detail":"After another six-mile march, Jackson’s infantry traveled about 34 miles by rail to Manassas Junction."},{"time":"24:31","audioTime":1471,"title":"Henry Hill creates the Stonewall name","detail":"The hosts summarize Jackson’s stand and initiative at First Manassas. Barnard Bee’s comparison of Jackson to a stone wall became attached to both commander and brigade."},{"time":"25:36","audioTime":1536,"title":"Jackson’s faith in the bayonet","detail":"The episode connects this preference to Mexican War doctrine, then explains that rifled muskets made a close bayonet finish increasingly difficult."},{"time":"27:35","audioTime":1656,"title":"Flank attack and concentration matter more","detail":"Jackson’s stated principle was to avoid heavy odds, strike an exposed part of the enemy, and use maneuver, secrecy, and surprise to defeat forces in detail."},{"time":"28:40","audioTime":1720,"title":"Jackson says farewell to the brigade","detail":"On November 4 about 1,800 men assembled near Bull Run. Jackson thanked the formation he had trained and led before leaving for independent command."},{"time":"31:08","audioTime":1868,"title":"Jackson travels back across the Blue Ridge","detail":"He rode to Manassas Junction, went by train through Front Royal to Strasburg, then rode the Valley Turnpike to Winchester before midnight."},{"time":"32:50","audioTime":1970,"title":"Independent command begins at Winchester","detail":"Early on November 5 Jackson notified Richmond that he had assumed command of the Valley district, setting the stage for the 1862 campaign."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Lt. Gen. Winfield Scott","command":"General-in-Chief, United States Army","subordinates":"Directed the separate forces under Patterson in the Shenandoah Valley and McDowell in northeastern Virginia"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Robert Patterson","command":"Army of the Shenandoah / Department of Pennsylvania","subordinates":"Crossed from Williamsport on July 2 and drove toward Martinsburg; failed to prevent Johnston from leaving the Valley for Manassas"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. John J. Abercrombie","command":"Lead brigade at Falling Waters","subordinates":"United States infantry and artillery pressed Jackson’s small force south from the Falling Waters area"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Irvin McDowell","command":"Department of Northeastern Virginia","subordinates":"Commanded the United States army defeated by Johnston and Beauregard at First Manassas"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Commander of Virginia forces","subordinates":"Issued guidance to Jackson about Maryland and provided the written authority by which Johnston assumed command at Harpers Ferry"},{"commander":"Gen. Joseph E. Johnston","command":"Army of the Shenandoah","subordinates":"Replaced Jackson at Harpers Ferry, withdrew to Winchester, ordered the reconnaissance at Falling Waters, and moved the army to Manassas"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Thomas J. Jackson","command":"First Brigade, Army of the Shenandoah","subordinates":"2nd, 4th, 5th, 27th, and 33rd Virginia Infantry and the Rockbridge Artillery; led the brigade until November 1861"},{"commander":"Col. Kenton Harper","command":"5th Virginia Infantry at Falling Waters","subordinates":"Reported 380 men engaged under Jackson on July 2; Maj. James W. Baylor directed two deployed companies"},{"commander":"Capt. William N. Pendleton","command":"Rockbridge Artillery","subordinates":"Brought a four-gun battery forward; one gun entered the Falling Waters action and covered the withdrawal"},{"commander":"Lt. Col. J. E. B. Stuart","command":"1st Virginia Cavalry detachment","subordinates":"Screened Jackson at Falling Waters, captured prisoners, and later screened Johnston’s movement through Ashby’s Gap"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. P. G. T. Beauregard","command":"Army of the Potomac at Manassas","subordinates":"Combined with Johnston’s arriving Valley army to defeat McDowell at First Manassas"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Organized the First Virginia Brigade, led it at Falling Waters and First Manassas, and ended the episode as commander of the Valley district","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep139-stonewall-jackson.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/"},{"name":"Joseph E. Johnston","role":"Assumed command at Harpers Ferry, formed the Army of the Shenandoah, and moved it by road and rail to reinforce Manassas","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"As commander of Virginia forces, warned Jackson about political sensitivity in Maryland and established Johnston’s authority at Harpers Ferry","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","role":"Scouted Patterson’s crossing, screened Jackson’s withdrawal, captured prisoners at Falling Waters, and covered Johnston’s July movement east","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Kenton Harper","role":"Commanded the 5th Virginia Infantry, the only Confederate infantry regiment engaged at Falling Waters","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William N. Pendleton","role":"Episcopal clergyman and artillery captain whose gun helped check the United States advance at Falling Waters","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Barnard E. Bee Jr.","role":"Brigade commander traditionally credited with giving Jackson and his brigade the “Stonewall” name at First Manassas; mortally wounded that day","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"P. G. T. Beauregard","role":"Commanded the Confederate army already at Manassas and fought the July 21 battle with Johnston’s reinforcements","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Robert Patterson","role":"Led the United States advance from Williamsport through Martinsburg but did not prevent Johnston from reinforcing Manassas","side":"United States"},{"name":"Irvin McDowell","role":"Commanded the United States army defeated at First Manassas after Johnston’s Valley troops reinforced Beauregard","side":"United States"},{"name":"John D. Imboden","role":"Artillery officer whose later recollections supplied descriptions of Jackson’s early discipline and the disputed large-scale B&O locomotive seizure","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Brig. Gen. Barnard E. Bee Jr.","side":"Confederacy","role":"Third Brigade, Army of the Shenandoah","outcome":"Mortally wounded on Henry Hill at First Manassas on July 21, 1861. His reported comparison of Jackson to a stone wall became the source of Jackson’s enduring nickname."},{"name":"Col. Francis S. Bartow","side":"Confederacy","role":"Second Brigade, Army of the Shenandoah","outcome":"Killed at First Manassas on July 21, 1861 while leading his brigade in the fighting associated with the Confederate defense and counterattack."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep139-stonewall-jackson.jpg","alt":"Photographic portrait of Confederate general Thomas J. Stonewall Jackson in uniform","caption":"A wartime portrait of Jackson. During the six months covered here, he rose from a Virginia colonel commanding at Harpers Ferry to a Confederate major general with an independent Valley district.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/"},{"image":"/images/ep139-harpers-ferry-1861.jpg","alt":"1861 photograph of Harpers Ferry after Confederate evacuation with the railroad bridge in ruins","caption":"Harpers Ferry photographed in 1861 after the Confederate evacuation. The ruined railroad bridge shows the direct connection between the town’s geography, the B&O, and the destruction accompanying military withdrawal.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2014646045/"},{"image":"/images/ep139-first-manassas-lithograph.jpg","alt":"Color lithograph of the Battle of Bull Run on July 21, 1861","caption":"A contemporary popular lithograph of First Manassas, published in 1861. It is an artistic overview rather than a reliable tactical diagram. Jackson’s brigade fought on Henry Hill, where the Stonewall name originated.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/91481737/"},{"image":"/images/ep139-harpers-ferry-falling-waters.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map of Harpers Ferry, Falling Waters, Martinsburg, and Winchester","caption":"The northern Valley corridor placed Harpers Ferry, the B&O Railroad, the Potomac crossings, Martinsburg, and Winchester in one connected operational problem.","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-2.pdf"},{"image":"/images/ep139-valley-to-manassas.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Jackson’s forced march and railroad journey from Winchester to Manassas","caption":"Johnston’s infantry used a forced march through Ashby’s Gap and the Manassas Gap Railroad to evade Patterson and reinforce Beauregard before First Manassas.","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-2.pdf"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 139 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/01/17/civil-war-podcast-episode-139/"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Civil War Begins, Opening Clashes, 1861","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-2.pdf"},{"label":"National Park Service: Thomas J. Jackson at Harpers Ferry","url":"https://www.nps.gov/hafe/learn/historyculture/thomas-j-jackson.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Maryland Heights","url":"https://www.nps.gov/hafe/learn/historyculture/maryland-heights.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: B&O bridge ruins at Harpers Ferry","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/harpers-ferry-baltimore-ohio-bridge-ruins.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: First Manassas battle narrative","url":"https://www.nps.gov/mana/learn/historyculture/first-manassas.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Confederate order of battle at First Manassas","url":"https://www.nps.gov/mana/learn/historyculture/confederate-order-of-battle-first-manassas.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Manassas I battle summary","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va005"},{"label":"Primary source: Jackson’s July 3, 1861 report on Falling Waters, from the Official Records","url":"https://spirit61.info/primary-sources/t-j-jackson-1/"},{"label":"Encyclopedia Virginia: Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","url":"https://encyclopediavirginia.org/entries/jackson-thomas-j-stonewall-1824-1863/"},{"label":"Encyclopedia Virginia: Harpers Ferry during the Civil War","url":"https://encyclopediavirginia.org/entries/harpers-ferry-during-the-civil-war/"},{"label":"Encyclopedia Virginia: Baltimore and Ohio Railroad during the Civil War","url":"https://encyclopediavirginia.org/entries/baltimore-and-ohio-railroad-during-the-civil-war/"},{"label":"Encyclopedia Virginia: Stonewall Brigade","url":"https://encyclopediavirginia.org/entries/stonewall-brigade/"},{"label":"Virginia Military Institute Museum: Little Sorrel in the collection","url":"https://www.vmi.edu/research/museums-and-archives/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Harpers Ferry after Confederate evacuation, 1861","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2014646045/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Battle of Bull Run lithograph, 1861","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/91481737/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: 1863 Harpers Ferry and environs map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448910/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: First Manassas, Henry Hill map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2006626053/"}]}}]}]
